<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Peureulak Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">840 CE – 1292 CE (approx.) · capital Peureulak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A port on the east coast of Aceh claimed by tradition as the first Muslim sultanate in Southeast Asia, and seen as Muslim by Marco Polo in 1292.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Local tradition, recorded in late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> whose authenticity is disputed, says a sultanate was founded at Perlak in 840 by a ruler of Arab descent. Historians treat this date with caution. Perlak lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and attracted Muslim merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The port exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and forest products and was a meeting place for Muslim traders. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> stopped at Ferlec in 1292 he reported that its townspeople had been converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by Saracen merchants, while the people of the hills were not.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Perlak declined in the late 13th century and was absorbed by the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samudera-pasai-sultanate">Samudera Pasai Sultanate</a>, tradition says through a royal marriage. Today the region is part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-aceh-regency">East Aceh</a>, and Indonesians often cite Perlak as the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> first took hold in the archipelago.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peureulak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Peureulak Sultanate" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>840 CE – 1292 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Peureulak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Acehnese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Perlak, Peureulak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1066&amp;focus=peureulak-sultanate" aria-label="Peureulak Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7393 1999 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Peureulak Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7713 2255L7695 2242"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7695 2242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7713 2255h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7713 2255h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Peureulak. <a href="/atlas?year=1066&amp;focus=peureulak-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
